// ==UserScript==
// @name         Murther — gota.io client (BETA)
// @namespace    murther.gota.beta
// @version      0.0.1
// @updateURL    https://raw.githubusercontent.com/REPLACE_USER/REPLACE_REPO/REPLACE_BRANCH/Murther%20Beta%20Version/murther.user.beta.js
// @downloadURL  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/REPLACE_USER/REPLACE_REPO/REPLACE_BRANCH/Murther%20Beta%20Version/murther.user.beta.js
// BETA-REL: set REPLACE_USER/REPLACE_REPO/REPLACE_BRANCH to your GitHub path, then
// testers must install once FROM that raw URL for Tampermonkey auto-update to work.
// @description  Murther - a full UI/UX replacement client for play.gota.io: a dark purple theme and a HUD reskin that HOSTS the live native panels (stats ID/Mass/Score/Cells top-centre, FPS/ping/server above the chat, leaderboard top-right, minimap, party, chat) so everything stays synced with the game; a native-synced server list with a verified pick -> join handshake; a clean name/mass leaderboard with an animated border that highlights your own row; searchable settings, themes and a full backup; client hotkeys with live write-through rebinding, chat macros and game-side action keys; and real performance controls (FPS cap / vsync governor, renderer resolution, reduce effects). A self-healing HUD keeps it honest: a state that would leave every panel hidden is reset once, with a toast, instead of blanking the screen. Feature rows explain themselves behind their own arrow (click it) instead of on hover - a category header is the only hover description left; the number on a category header is the real count of rows it is showing; Themes opens with Enable Custom Theme, which switches the client's whole custom look off and says so; Play and Spectate wear an animated white outline; and the profile card's particle field is fitted to the real device pixels, reacts to the pointer and demotes itself when frames get slow.
// @description  Every release note and the full behavioural history live in the RELEASE HISTORY block below the header - the metadata above carries only the current feature set, so it can never go stale or outgrow a userscript manager's UI.
// @author       Murther
// @match        https://play.gota.io/*
// @run-at       document-start
// @grant        none
// @noframes
// ==/UserScript==
//
// ============================== RELEASE HISTORY ==============================
// v1.44.0 moved the changelog out of the metadata header, where it had grown to 19 KB:
// userscript managers render @description in their own UI, so a header that size was
// unreadable there and unmaintainable here. The notes are kept verbatim, oldest first,
// with the older feature prose at the end of the block. The live metadata block is the
// one directly above this text; the leftover pre-v1.44.0 header lines further down the
// file are plain comments.
//
// Legacy feature prose (kept verbatim): Dark purple theme, full HUD reskin that hosts the LIVE native panels (stats ID/Mass/Score/Cells top-center, connection FPS/ping/server above the chat, leaderboard top-right, minimap, party, chat) so everything stays synced with the game. Self-healing HUD: a zeroed-opacity theme or a blackout state that would leave every panel hidden is reset once (with a toast) instead of silently blanking the screen. Native-synced server list (players/bots) with a watertight pick → join handshake (the highlighted server is re-verified against the game before every join). Ryuten-style leaderboard with a border highlight while your name is up. Client-level hotkeys (searchable) that list the game's own gameplay keybinds with live write-through rebinding, chat macros (Alt+1..0) and a split-indicator helper. Game notification banners can be toggled off in Settings; every slider value renders as a readable badge. FPS cap runs as a page-wide vsync governor and "Uncap FPS" also removes the engine's own Ticker limiter for the real maximum. Graphics quality now drives the captured renderer's resolution (Low to Retina, applies live). A Settings searchbar filters every section (Themes: "Search theme..."). The Dot Position Crosshair can hide its X/Y readouts. Leaderboard glow is party-and-self only (extra-name lists are gone); the minimap draws the arena's own A1/B1 sector callouts at its exact size; Pastel mode and arena Borders render for real (CSS filter + fx overlay). 
//
// Release notes, oldest first:
//
// v1.29.0: the stats strip never freezes (the native score panel is ghosted and mirrored live at ~150ms), the minimap draws your nick + marker at your true position with live x/y coordinates in a display font, and the Server/FPS/Ping chips have clear spacing. 
// v1.30.0: mouse wheel zoom works again in-game (the engine ignores synthetic wheel events, so the real trusted event is now amplified in place instead of being replaced), Esc leaves the game cleanly and you stay dead while the menu is open (Auto Respawn is parked there, Spectate is verified), the HUD panels stay visible behind the menu, Themes are always on with working Blur/Opacity, the leaderboard is client-rendered with themed animated rows, chat animation can be switched off, and the menu's right pane never moves when you change category. 
// v1.31.0: the wheel is a real curve (100% = the game's step, up to 600%) with a short flick ramp. 
// v1.32.0: the wheel finally uses BOTH real levers - the game's own Zoom Speed option is raised to its maximum once (and stays live-linked), the sensitivity curve is a real 4x the engine's step at the 150% default with a floor that makes high-resolution wheels count and a live \"last notch\" readout to tune against, and the Server status dot sits clear of its label. 
// v1.33.0: the chat panel's All / Party tab labels carry the client's own tab typography (uppercase, heavy, letter-spaced) exactly like the menu tabs - text only, no layout or behaviour change. 
// v1.34.0: the native bridge actually finds the new game's controls (id first, then label - the old label pass skipped every row that declared an id, which made Show Skins and Hide Food unreachable), reads/writes by the control's real type instead of the declared one, queues a toggle pressed before the game's panel exists instead of bouncing it back, and the arena border is now Murther's own overlay so "Show borders" always works; a Copy native controls dump button reports the game's real markup. 
// v1.36.0: the native bridge now reads the game's real settings markup (label text node, dependency-disabled rows) and matches on the label alone so a description can no longer hijack another row, writes follow each control's actual shape (word, option position or clamped number) and fire input+change so a range editor cannot revert them, Camera delay / Animation delay / View distance read back from the native control, new Camera focus and Independent zoom rows, Menu delay is a real Murther feature (wait then fade), and Hotkeys mirrors the game's own keybinds read-only. 
// v1.37.0: the stats strip (Mass/Score/Cells/ID) reads THREE sources - the writes the engine itself puts into its stats panel (hooked setters, so a write is caught even when the engine holds a node that was replaced), the panel's live text (labels matched by meaning across the client's languages, then by the panel's own ID/Mass/Score/Cells order), and the mass the engine draws on your own cell - and the native panel is parked IN PLACE instead of being hidden or flown off-screen, because a panel with no layout box is what let the engine's self-rescheduling stats writer stop and leave its template "Mass: 0" for the strip to mirror forever. 
// v1.37.1: the settings bridge labels a row BY SHAPE - any node whose class mentions "label", with the row's own description excluded (by class, a small tag or Murther's hint class) so an explanation that repeats another row's wording can no longer hijack a matcher - it treats any row class containing "disabled" as dependency-disabled, scans .settings-section-row and .options-panel-shell children too, and exposes __murther.nativeSet(key, value) to exercise one native write on its own. 
// v1.38.0: two new tabs host the GAME'S OWN editors instead of re-implementing them - Appearance (the look/skin editor: My cells / Friends / Bots / Enemies, the skin-link field, colours, name styles, saved looks) and Theme (the theme editor: World / Interface / Grid / Objects / Effects / Presets, food colours, virus and mothercell skins, tracers, the line-split guide) - by adopting the game's real panel nodes into the menu (moved, never cloned, so every native control keeps working), restyled onto the client's accent through the game's own --hud-* variables, with a reversible adoption (leaving the tab, closing the menu or rebuilding the shell hands the panel back exactly where the game left it), a whitelisted and rationed entry-button probe for panels the game renders lazily, a Copy panels dump for builds whose markup differs, hosting for the image links (imgur page links normalised to their direct URL, imgur/skin-data.gota.io links verified and cached locally so a blocked hotlink still previews) and a named look library that rides the Backup export. 
// v1.38.1: the game's own UI Scale finally does something visible in this client - the row ADOPTS the serial the game's control really uses (a fraction range, a percent range or a preset list) instead of unioning Murther's span with the game's, which had turned a 50..150 control into a 0.5..150 slider whose badge read "100.00x" and whose writes the engine's clamp collapsed to the minimum, and the factor the game holds is applied to the panels Murther renders itself (stats strip, leaderboard, minimap, chat shell, party), each around the corner the game anchors it to, because the engine can only scale its own copies and this client hides, redraws or transform-neutralises those; bridged rows also follow the game's own panels once a tick, __murther.uiScale() reports the control it found and the factor it applied, and the UI-scale fixture (a shape-cycling stub) exercises all three serials. 
// v1.39.0: the strip under the leaderboard is a real FOUR-COLUMN grid - Reset / Respawn / Time / Spec, each feature in its own column with its own label, a wider card and per-column ellipsis, so a long value can only ever be clipped instead of running into its neighbour - and the game's own extra strip (Reset / Spectators / Respawn / Client version) is PARKED IN PLACE at zero opacity with its layout box kept, instead of being display:none'd, which is exactly what let its "Respawn:" row reappear painted over the footer; the board also animates PLACE changes now: a drop makes the row fall with a short spring overshoot, an amber wash, a fading ghost bar of where it came from and a slide-in delta chip, a gain mirrors it in green, your own row gets the strongest treatment plus one warn-coloured border pulse on the card, places come from the rank token the game itself drew, a row's first sighting never fires a delta, and Reduce effects still renders all of it static. __murther.lbFoot() reports the four cells and what the native strip is doing. 
// v1.40.0: the stats strip stops depending on the game's own #score-panel - the native client does not update that panel in this build (it reads 0 there too), so the numbers now come from the LIVE scene: Murther already holds the Pixi Application (captured through Pixi's own __PIXI_APP_INIT__ hook), so the mass the engine draws on YOUR own cell is read as the Text node's own text - the numeric label nearest the screen centre, stable across frames, only in a live match - with the panel, the engine's own writes and the canvas kept as ordered fallbacks and the winning source reported per number; a Settings > Camera scene probe lists every numeric text in the scene with its screen position, so a wrong number is a measurement instead of a guess. The settings bridge also stops claiming a row by a loose regex: a row is claimed by a label the setting OWNS (so Show Mass can no longer bind the game's Mass Type display control, or Show Skins its auto-skin-hiding switch), an ambiguous label is refused and reported rather than written, and every native write is verified by reading the control straight back and undone when the game did not accept it. 
// v1.41.0: the stats strip reads the engine's own numbers WHEREVER they land - a LEDGER records every short numeric write that does not go into the parked panel together with the label that names it (Mass / Score / Cells / ID), those writes outrank the panel's text, and a panel still showing exactly the markup it showed on first sight is recognised as the engine's TEMPLATE and can never be a source again, because "Mass: 0" outliving the game was the whole report; the mass on YOUR own cell is no longer guessed from the screen centre but found STRUCTURALLY - the engine draws a cell's name and its mass as two Text siblings under one cell container, so the numeric sibling of a label carrying your own nick is your mass (anchored frame after frame, even on a smoothing camera) - and the SUM of those labels is the score the client itself posts into its HUD; a Settings switch keeps the game's own "Show mass" on so that label exists (bounded: at most three times a session, only in a live match, only when that control says it is off), a Copy stats ledger button dumps the out-of-panel writes with the element each went into, and nothing is invented - a value no source can answer stays unknown. 
// v1.41.1: the minimap coordinate readout finally TRACKS you - it used to depend on one thing, app.stage.position, and sat on a frozen "x:0 y:0" whenever that was not the camera, which is the case on this build because the engine translates its WORLD container and leaves the stage alone. The game's own #minimap-coordinates is read first (hooked writes plus a text poll, in the engine's own units), then your own cell container in the captured scene, with the legacy camera maths kept only as a last resort and REFUSED when the stage is never translated - so the readout shows "—" instead of a plausible constant when nothing can answer, one resolver feeds the readout AND the minimap self marker, and __murther.pos() reports every source. A "Show coordinates" switch (Settings > HUD & Windows) owns the readout, so Reduce effects can no longer hide it while you play. The sector callouts are the game's own A1/B1 shape - rows lettered A.. downwards, columns numbered 1.. rightwards, a large muted label in every sector, six by six by default - and they are LABELS ONLY: no lines and no outer frame are drawn, because the old grid stroked a frame in #FF0000 (the red border); lines stay available as "Labels + lines" with their own colour and thickness, Sector Style/Columns/Rows/Labels/Label colour/Label size are real rows, one migration rewrites the old factory red + 5x5 + labels-off values, and the readout carries your sector (C4 · x:1234 y:-567) so map and readout always agree. 
// v1.42.0: the stats strip finally reads the game's OWN stats panel the way the game writes it - BY ITS OWN IDS (#pmass span, #pscore span / #playerScore, #pcells, #pid) - which is where Mass and Score have been all along, instead of matching labels in twenty languages or trusting a positional ID/Mass/Score/Cells order this panel does not use (its order is id, fps, ping, mass, score, cells); the read is made trustworthy by remembering each value's first reading and letting the panel LEAD only once it has been seen to MOVE - a panel that has never moved in a session is the engine's dead template and is refused outright (so "stuck at 0" is impossible rather than unlikely), while one that has moved can no longer be rejected by the template rule at all; Cells keeps the client's own n/cap form, a value that does not start with a number ("?/64", which this build really renders) stays unknown rather than being read as the cap, the source order is native-by-id then the engine's hooked writes then a labelled ledger write anywhere else on the page then the panel text then the live scene, and the stats/ledger/scene diagnostics all report the id map with each node, its raw text, its first and current value and whether anything ever moved. Kept from v1.41.0: a LEDGER of every short numeric write that does not land in the parked panel, with the label that names it, outranking the panel's text; the mass on your own cell found STRUCTURALLY rather than by screen position (the numeric Text sibling of the label carrying your nick), with the SUM of those labels as the score the client itself posts into its HUD; a bounded switch that keeps the game's own "Show mass" on so that label exists; and a Copy stats ledger button that names the element each value came from. 
// v1.42.1: the board footer's four columns are verified clip-free in BOTH layouts (4x62px inside the 248px card, 2x84px in the compact 170px one, longest real value 09:29:59, no cell ever overlaps another), and the game's RE-CREATED extra strip now carries the WHOLE invisible stance from the stylesheet itself - visibility:hidden on top of opacity:0, and still never display:none so its layout box survives for the engine's own HUD writer - instead of depending on a tick to re-park it, which was the only window in which its "Respawn:" row could ever be seen over the footer; the parking tick re-asserts on visibility too, so a strip the game rebuilds is re-parked and re-tracked in the same pass. The footer parser is fixed for the same report: a column's value now ends at the NEXT LABEL (or a newline, or the end of the text) instead of at a 24-character budget, because the game's strip is ONE flat text node with no newlines in it - "Reset: 15:09 Spectators: 0 Respawn: 00:00 Client version: 1.42" - so Respawn used to render "00:00 Client version: 1" and run into its neighbour; a capture that would still run on into a label the client does not know is refused (the column shows its dash and says so in its tooltip) rather than guessed, and the harness ships __mxFootCheck(), which swaps that exact flat shape in and asserts the columns, plus the refusal case. 
// v1.42.2: the sector callouts are laid out properly - the label is CENTRED on its cell instead of parked in the cell's top-left corner with a pad, and its baseline is taken from the LOADED font's own ink metrics so the ink box (not the em box) lands on the centre whatever font resolves, one measurement serving the whole grid rather than a per-cell measureText at 30-60fps; the auto size is fitted to the cell on both axes and sits one pixel-grid step lower than the old 0.42 factor (24px to 16px at the shipped 6x6 on a 360px minimap, ink 26x15 shrinking to 15x10, still snapped to 8s so the pixel font stays crisp and still overridable by Themes > Label size), so a label can no longer spill at any Columns/Rows; and the line lattice now fills the ENTIRE minimap - it runs 0..cols / 0..rows, so the outer boundary is stroked as well instead of leaving every edge sector unbounded, each line spanning the whole buffer with the outer ones half a pixel inside it so they are fully inked rather than half-clipped (6x6 goes from 10 internal separators to 14 lines); __murther.state().sectorGrid reports the resolved labelPx and cellPx so the fit and the centring are checkable in one call. 
// v1.42.2b [SUPERSEDED by v1.43.0 (the tabs were removed) and v1.44.0 (the hosting engine is gated off) - nothing below can apply, kept for the record]: the Appearance and Theme tabs now showed the game’s OWN look by default. Those two tabs host the game’s own editors, and the client used to repaint them - it forced its font on every descendant, wrote its accent into the game’s own --hud-* variables, restyled every input and stripped every card’s background - so the hosted panel’s typography, colours, spacing and layout all read as Murther’s instead of the game’s, which is exactly what the game’s own menu does NOT look like. Every one of those rules now sits behind body.mx-panels-themed: with the switch off (the default) the hosted subtree receives no client rule at all and the game’s stylesheet alone decides how its panel looks, while the dock keeps only two structural rules (full width with sideways scroll instead of a squeezed single column, and a font-family measured from the GAME’s own document, because a directly-set family always beats the one inherited from the client shell). Settings > Themes ships the switch (Native look for the game’s own panels, on by default, applies live, rides the Themes backup group) and __murther.panelLook(on) flips it from the console. The hosted panel is also lowered into inert copies of its REAL ancestors (mxPanelChain) - id and classes only, layout neutralised, dropped the moment the panel goes home - because the game’s stylesheet is full of ancestor-scoped rules (#main-themes .options-table input, #subpanel-content .color-setting__control) that a node moved out of its chain stops matching, which is how a hosted editor ends up with rows that look nothing like the game’s. __murther.panels() reports look: native|themed. 
// v1.43.0: the menu is FIVE categories - the Appearance and Theme tabs are gone, together with the panes and the host wiring that adopted the game's own look and theme editors into them (those editors remain in the game's own menu; the hosting module stays in the file, dormant and unreachable, rather than being deleted out from under whoever edits it next), so no category hosts a native panel any more; the leaderboard footer loses its Respawn column and is now Reset / Time / Spec in THREE tracks that fit one row in both card sizes with room to spare (the measured worst case, an 8-character clock, needs 43.9px against the compact card's 56.7px track), while the game's own Respawn row is still parsed and STAYS in the parser's label vocabulary, because it is what ends the Spectators capture - dropping it there would leave Spec showing its dash forever; and the server list's players column is CENTRED on the row, with the two outer tracks mirrored (1fr | players | 1fr), which moves the column's axis from 70px right of the row's midpoint to exactly on it and widens the mode's track from 92px to 166px in the compact card, so long mode names stop ellipsising. __murther.lbFoot() reports the three footer columns.
//
// v1.44.0: a review pass over the whole client. (1) The Donut-derived game-side actions
// could never run: they sat in no Hotkeys section, no label map and no dispatch line, so
// nothing could list, bind or fire them, and two cases called freezeToggle()/freezeStart()
// - neither of which exists in this file - or leaned on synthetic key events the engine
// ignores. What genuinely can work is now wired: show/hide skins, show/hide names,
// show/hide mass, show/hide food, Auto Decline, Auto Respawn, leave the match and
// queue/join a scrimmage appear under Hotkeys > "Game display & match", are searchable and
// bindable, are dispatched from the key handler, and each drives the game's OWN control (a
// native select cycled, a native checkbox toggled, the game's own button clicked) - and
// each says so instead of doing nothing when a build no longer ships that option. Their
// labels and help come from one table, CLIENT_ACTIONS, so a new one can never be added
// without being listed again. The five that only duplicated an existing Murther toggle
// were dropped, and with them the now-unused toggleMurtherPanel and the never-read
// donutFreezeMode setting. (2) Six functions nothing could reach were deleted: lbNameList,
// resetThemeSettings, addText, mmNamesHost, mmSelfWorld and the singular nativeOptionsRoot
// (mxPanelBuild is deliberately kept - it belongs to the engine below). (3) The
// hosted-panel engine (the v1.38.0 Appearance/Theme adoption) is GATED behind
// MX_HOST = false: since those tabs were removed nothing could enter it, yet its 1 Hz
// tick, its menu re-arm, its retry path and its console API all still implied that
// hosting a game panel was a live feature, and its "Native look for the game's own
// panels" Settings switch could not change anything on screen - that row is gone, the
// engine is inert at zero cost, and re-enabling it is one flag plus one nav entry.
// __murther.panels() now reports gated: true.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// v1.45.0: DevTools no longer looks like a broken client. The game's chunks ship ~1,885
// `debugger` statements (487 in one 181 KB chunk) that pause the page whenever DevTools is
// open with breakpoints active, plus domain-lock IIFEs of the shape
// `if(<host check>){while(true){}}` - so opening the console used to freeze the HUD. The
// honest part first: NO page script can strip those traps before they run. This app is a
// Vite ES-module build (__vitePreload + import("./chunk.js")), so its chunks arrive through
// the browser's module loader, which fetch/XHR wrappers and createElement('script') hooks
// never see, and Chrome exposes no page API for Deactivate breakpoints / Pause on exceptions
// / Never pause here. Wrapping fetch is therefore deliberately NOT installed: it would only
// cover classic script loaders, which this build does not use, so it would add risk and buy
// nothing. What the client does instead is do the work of the hand-made override recipe and
// catch the freeze while it happens: (1) a code-context-only cleaner (mxAD.clean) that walks
// the source as a state machine - comments, strings, template `${}` nesting, regex vs
// division - edits ONLY code context, records every span and can rebuild the original
// byte-for-byte from those spans (mxAD.restore), so "changed = original minus the recorded
// traps" is a checked property rather than a claim; a plain `s/debugger//g` would corrupt the
// chunks that carry the word inside strings, and this build does; (2) an on-demand scanner
// that seeds from this page's own module scripts and modulepreload links, follows the literal
// `import()` references it finds, and reports per file how many traps there are, whether the
// cleaned text still compiles and which files actually need an override - same-origin reads
// only, nothing executed, nothing sent anywhere, never at boot; (3) one "Download cleaned
// copy" button per affected file, named exactly as the original so it drops over the local
// Overrides copy, one click per file so Chrome never asks about multi-downloads; (4) a FREEZE
// GUARD in the rAF loop: a >1.2 s frame gap on a visible, booted page (after an 8 s boot
// grace, and ignoring gaps that contain a visibilitychange) counts as a stall and tells you
// once to press Ctrl+F8, with the counters on __murther.devtools() and in the new Settings >
// Developer console section (Freeze guard toggle, Scan this build, Copy help, live status).
// The domain-lock loops are neutralised in the cleaned copies too, which is what saves the
// client on any host other than play.gota.io - there the domain check already passes.
// Also fixed here: the section-header row-count regex was missing both of its backslashes
// (the middot-joined count pattern), so it never stripped a previous count.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// v1.45.1: the menu nav buttons (Servers / Settings / Hotkeys / Themes / Backup) are readable
// at a glance. The base .mx-tab rule shipped at font-size 8px with 7px 2px padding, and the
// strip was using 181.2px of the 260px it has, so there was room that the old comment - which
// claimed "five uppercase labels plus icons cannot fit a 240px card" - had written off. Sized
// from real text metrics instead of a guess: 10px/800 with 7px 3px padding needs 229.5px in the
// tightest real box (a 921px-wide window, where 30vw leaves 240.3px), i.e. 10.5px of slack,
// where 11px (268.6px) and 10px with 5px side padding (249.5px) both truncate a label. Vertical
// padding is unchanged, so nothing above the nav moves; the tab grows only by its line box
// (~2.6px, absorbed by the card's 56px bottom padding) and the sliding pill follows on its own
// because syncNavPill measures the tab's real box. No JS was touched.
//
// v1.46.0: the freeze is no longer something you diagnose, it is something you finish. The guard
// used to call every long gap a stall; it now splits the two causes, because they need different
// words and a different order of hand. A gap of 8s or more cannot be a slow frame - DevTools is
// HOLDING the page on a debugger trap (the reported case was 44.7s) - so that reads "Press F8 to
// resume, then Ctrl+F8: with breakpoints off the statement is a no-op", while a 1.2-8s gap stays a
// genuine stall with no key to press. A sticky sessionStorage flag keeps the one-per-load limit
// honest across a reload, and a second notice fires BEFORE the next freeze, only when there is
// evidence (docked DevTools, measured as outerWidth-innerWidth > 200, or a pause already seen this
// session), never once the overrides are proven active - clicking it copies the whole recipe.
// The recipe itself was re-ordered by what actually works: Ctrl+F8 first (verified), then the
// DevTools Ignore List with one regex for the whole site ('^https://play\.gota\.io/.*\.js$' - a
// debugger pause is skipped when EVERY frame in the stack is ignored, which is why one pattern for
// all the site's scripts works where a single file may not), and "Pause on exceptions" demoted to
// a footnote since it only matters when the game throws on purpose.
// The permanent fix is now one gesture. "Install overrides" asks for your Overrides folder once
// (the handle is kept in IndexedDB, because DevTools only applies overrides on the NEXT load),
// writes each cleaned chunk to <folder>/<host>/<name>.js, and READS EVERY FILE BACK - a resolved
// write is not proof, so a silent write failure cannot pass as success. "Remove overrides" deletes
// them again. And because the scan re-reads the same URLs through the overrides, the status line
// can PROVE the fix instead of asking for trust: the previous build's trap count is stashed before
// each scan (never overwritten with a zero) and compared, so "Overrides ACTIVE: 0 traps now, 1,885
// before" is a measurement, not a promise. Settings > Developer console gained the installer's own
// status line, the pause counters and the attached-DevTools line; the console API gained
// __murther.antidebug.installOverrides/removeOverrides/attached/overridesActive/ignorePattern.
// What has NOT changed is the limit, and the help text says so plainly: the running page can never
// be cleaned - the app is a Vite ES-module build whose chunks arrive through the browser's module
// loader - so the overrides folder remains the only route that removes the traps before they run.
//
// v1.47.0: joining a server works again, and the reason it did not is a lesson about evidence. The
// pre-join guard asked the game "is the row you selected the one the user picked?" and refused to
// join when it could not confirm - but it read the answer from the FIRST .server-selected in the
// document and demanded PAINTED .server-row nodes. Both are wrong in this build. The game can hold
// more than one row to a name and more than one list at a time (the stylesheet ships a per-region
// table, a loading state with .server-list-skeleton-row and a .server-list-status.is-loading
// footer, i.e. the rows are replaced while the list reloads), so with two lists the guard could
// read the OTHER table's selection, decide the pick was never accepted, try again, fail the same
// way and cancel - forever, on a pick the game had taken. Murther's mirror kept the rows (it leaves
// them alone while the native list is empty), which is why the list looked perfect while every join
// was refused. The handshake now: resolves rows from every shape the list has shipped (row class,
// table rows inside #server-content/#main-servers, and - last resort - anything carrying a name
// cell), never rejects a candidate for being unpainted (painted rows are merely preferred), reads
// the selection INSIDE the table that holds the pick, compares names after collapsing whitespace,
// selects through the realistic pointer sequence every other native forward uses, and VERIFIES by
// polling the game's own selection instead of testing once. Three outcomes, each said out loud:
// confirmed (join), contradicted by a list that IS present (cancel - the invariant is untouched: a
// game that refuses the pick still cannot be joined), or a list that is not there at all, which is
// a REFRESH and now joins with "Game list was still loading - joining X anyway" instead of the
// old dead end. The pick itself also reports a refusal at click time now, where the user just
// clicked, and a post-join safety net compares the server the client actually connected to (the
// game's own readout in the connection strip) against the pick, so an unverifiable join is still
// checked once it lands. Diagnostics: __murther.serverCheck() / .serverSelect(name) list every
// candidate row, which are painted, and every table's selection - one paste answers "why".
// v1.48.0: auto-reverse is IN the client, not a companion script. mxReverseInstall()
// (wired with the other document-start sensors) wraps WebSocket.prototype.send exactly
// once - the __murtherWsHook flag and the origSend.__murtherHook stamp make a userscript
// re-run, or a second wrapping client, unable to double-negate the tick into a no-op -
// and rewrites the 9-byte input tick (10 <int32 X LE> <int32 Y LE>) in place when the
// mode is on: the engine's own send path ships the negated X/Y, so no synthetic input
// exists to trip isTrusted and the bytes are gone before the socket sees them. The
// toggle lives on window.__murtherReverse (on / flips / at) and binds like any client
// action: Hotkeys > Client features > "Auto-reverse (rewrite input tick)", toast and
// !e.repeat guard included; __murther.state().reverse reports installed/on/flips so "is
// it working" is one console call. Frame logging stays quiet by design - the counters
// always run, the per-frame [WS OUT] dump waits for __murtherReverse.verbose = true.
// v1.49.0: coexistence with gota-ws-hook (GotaWSHook/), the companion send-hook client, which
// - audited, not guessed - froze input in normal play: its freezeMode ships bound to KeyF, the
// GAME's Sextuple Split, and its freeze transform writes 0,0 into every outgoing tick, while
// its own AUTO REVERSE switch rendered "MX_ACTION.enabled && on" and so read DISABLED with the
// input already pinned - the first click left it dead. The client now releases the hook's
// gameplay-key binds through the hook's OWN api on load (persisted by the hook), always arms
// plain reverse, names any foreign mode, and repairs it from the hotkey; ownership of the send
// hook is decided by the wrapper STAMPS (__murtherHook / __mxHook) so exactly one negation can
// happen in any load order, replacing the shared __murtherWsHook flag the two scripts used for
// two different meanings; the client publishes its DevTools-trap evidence (trapAt /
// devtoolsSuspected / devguard) so the hook's 1.5s stall guard stops shedding auto-reverse for
// the game's own debugger traps; it publishes __murtherKeys so the hook yields a key the client
// owns; the hook's panel is tiered below the client HUD and parked while its menu is open; and
// the Hotkeys tab gains a "WS hook" card (state, released binds, Repair auto-reverse, the
// hook's pending key picker). __murther.hook() / .hookRelease() / .hookRepair() are the
// console handles and __murther.state().reverse now reports owner/mode/danger/shed.
// v1.50.0: the DevTools freeze is something the client FIXES instead of something it asks about, and the
// fix is PROVED before a byte is written. Measured on the live build: 1,885 `debugger` statements and 332
// self-check guards of the shape `if (HASH(T,seed) === CONST) { return T(...) } else { <empty spin> }`.
// Stripping only the traps - what every earlier version did - leaves each guard hashing a CHANGED function,
// so the guard takes its poison branch and the tab freezes for good (or, with the spin neutralised, the
// obfuscator's own string-table delegate returns undefined and the game breaks quietly). Compensating the
// hash helper cannot work either: the constants were computed from the text WITH the traps, so dropping the
// word shifts 232 of the 332. What does work is DEFUSING - the guard's condition becomes a constant true
// while its valid branch stays byte-identical, so nothing verifies a cleaned function any more. Proven
// offline against all 15 saved chunks (332/332 defused, branches kept verbatim, 0 residual guards, restore
// proof intact, every chunk still parses as an ES module) and enforced live per build: a file is written
// only with 0 residual guards, otherwise the install is refused and says why. The client also arms it by
// itself - with the Overrides folder remembered and permission still granted it rewrites only when the build
// changed, triggered by a survived pause or attached DevTools - and the sticky "press Ctrl+F8" notice is
// gone: one state line per session, one click only when the browser needs a gesture, and a remembered done
// for the file-free DevTools route - with the way back from it in Settings > Developer console ("Show the
// hint again"), because one click there would otherwise hide the only thing that explains a frozen page
// forever. Removing the overrides also drops the armed stamp, so the client cannot stay quiet about a
// problem it no longer fixes. A survived hold resets the governor clock, the rate window and the parked
// panels, so the frames after a 44s pause are not poisoned by it, and an explicit notice probe is never
// swallowed by the per-frame throttle (which is what made "the notice did not appear" depend on the frame
// it was asked in): the throttle exists for the app's own frame, a deliberate call always evaluates.
//
// v1.51.0: the menu pass - the nav is sized from measurements, and it can no longer be cut.
// The truncated labels were NOT a lack of room. The profile card was flex:0 1 auto with a 220px
// floor, and a flex item's base size is its own content size, so the settings pane (max-content
// measured at 2855px, off its search row) squeezed the card from 296px to that floor on EVERY tab
// except Servers - the strip fell from 264px to 188px and ellipsised all five labels ("Settings"
// is 40px of ink in a 28px box). .mx-left is flex:0 0 auto now: the card keeps its
// clamp(240px,30vw,296px) on every category, the right window absorbs the difference down to its
// own 380px floor (measured: every pane lays out at 544px with zero overflowing descendants), and
// the right card is finally the same 570px wide on all five tabs, which is what v1.30.0 always
// claimed. The buttons are then sized from the real text: at 11px/800 with 8px 2px padding the
// five labels need 253px of the 264px the strip has in a normal window (10px needs 239, 12px
// needs 272 and would demand a 1030px-wide window), the tab grows 44px -> 48px while the track's
// own padding gives back 4px, so the strip is 54px tall either way - measured A/B, nothing above
// the nav moves by even a pixel. A first pass at 3px of side padding needed 267px of 264 and was
// caught by the ruler before it shipped: it would have turned the nav into a list in a normal
// window, to show BIGGER buttons. And the labels can never be cut again, in any
// window: a hidden ruler (the strip's own markup, out of flow, always measured in row metrics)
// reports what the five labels need against what the strip has, and when they do not fit the nav
// becomes an animated list - FLIP, so each button glides from its old box to its new one while
// the track animates its height, with the label on the left and its glyph sliding in from the
// right on hover (always visible on the active row), and the pill following the active row down
// the other axis. Two pixels of hysteresis stop a resize from flapping. The players column also
// gains room in both layouts (142px -> 166px, 108px -> 130px): the centred count and the
// right-anchored BOT chip overlapped by 3.5px on the worst pair (119/200* + BOT x300) and cleared
// the reported one by 2px. Measured after the change: that worst pair now clears by 10.1px and the
// reported shape of row by 17.7px (14.6px with a 60-bot chip, 33-36px on the short counts), and
// 11px in the compact layout, which also narrows the chip's own padding by 1px rather than taking
// more room. The cost is real and stated: 12px from each of the two name/mode tracks, and exactly
// one long name in the harness' 14 stub rows now ellipsises - which is why the name cell's tooltip
// leads with the full name from this version on, so nothing becomes unreadable. __mxNavCheck() and
// __mxRowCheck() in test/preview.html hold all of it: the card's width on every tab, the
// never-cut labels in both layouts, the FLIP's exactness on the first frame, the pill in both
// axes, and the tracks plus every count/chip gap.
//
// v1.52.0: the menu pass on the three editor panes. The description that only Themes had now
// opens Settings and Hotkeys too, above the search bar. Every feature row is a CARD — a tinted
// header strip per category plus a bordered row carrying that category's own colour as a 2px
// accent — because "which of these am I changing" was unanswerable against a flat list of rows
// on one background; all three row shapes are covered (Settings .mx-set-row, Hotkeys .mx-kb,
// Themes .mx-trow) and the styling is scoped to a section's own body, so a row that lives
// inside a note card does not become a card inside a card. And a category is MOVABLE: the grip
// the stylesheet reserved (mx-tsec-grip / .dragging / .drag-over) is finally wired, HTML5 drag
// live-reorders a CLOSED category among the closed ones exactly like an open one, the order is
// written only on dragend (an interrupted drag can never persist half an arrangement), sorting
// is restricted to siblings so a drop in Settings cannot reach a category in Hotkeys, and
// dragging is refused while a search filter is active — a filtered list holds only the matches,
// so saving it would silently drop every hidden category's position. The arrangement is
// store-backed now: order AND open/closed live in S.menuLayout, ride the Settings backup group
// (the old open/closed flag lived only in localStorage, which is why no backup ever carried
// it), survive every pane rebuild (search keystroke, reset, import, native-panel flip), and are
// migrated once so nothing already collapsed re-opens itself. One real bug fell out of the
// keys: the Hotkeys "Game keybinds" section keyed itself on the live bind count, so its
// remembered open/closed state reset whenever the game's bind list changed.
//
// v1.52.1: two defects the live check caught before they shipped. The cards were scoped to a
// section's BODY, which is where Settings and Hotkeys anchor their rows — but Themes anchors its
// rows on the SECTION itself (.mx-tsec > .mx-trow), so every Themes row silently kept the old
// flat look, which was the pane the pass was for. Both parents are covered now, and the note
// cards' own .mx-set-row action strips stay uncarded on purpose: a card inside a card is not a
// feature. And a drag target with NO layout box (a 0x0 rect, which a synthesized or mid-rebuild
// drop can produce) made the midpoint test read "after" for every pointer position, so a move
// could land on the wrong side of a category the pointer never passed; no box now means no guess
// — the drop stays before the target. __mxMenuCheck() is the fixture that found both, and it is
// corrected too: the card assertion compares the accent against the row's own other borders
// (the shell carries zoom: 0.94, so a literal "2px" reads as 1.06px and a literal assertion is a
// false negative), the description is read from the search bar's OWN parent with the lookup
// scoped to the pane that is not .mx-hidden (Hotkeys and Themes wrap their categories in
// .mx-theme-body, so "is the intro above the bar" cannot be answered from the section's parent),
// the note-card probe looks where that card actually lives (it is not in Settings) and reports
// skipped rather than passing vacuously, its restore reorders sections PAIRWISE among themselves
// so a non-section sibling can never be displaced (the old fixed-slot restore wedged the intro
// and the search box into the middle of the category list), and its drag waits for real layout
// and computes the midpoint from the target's live rect.
//
// v1.53.0: the menu pass, part two - and it opens with a defect that was already on disk.
// mxDescAttach had lost its closing brace, so mxSectionNote ended up nested INSIDE it: every call
// site (two in Settings) resolved to an undefined name at runtime, which is a ReferenceError in
// the middle of buildSettingsView. Fixed first, because the rest of this version is what that
// helper is for.
//
// (1) DESCRIPTIONS ARE CLICK-TO-REVEAL, ONE ARROW PER ROW. A feature's how-to no longer lives in a
// hover tooltip. Settings and Themes already had theirs behind the arrow; Hotkeys was the last pane
// still explaining itself on hover, so what an action does (ACTION_HELP, fed by CLIENT_ACTIONS) and
// how to rebind it now open from the row's own arrow, and the key button's tooltip is its STATE -
// the bind it holds, the position that key sits in, any conflict it has - which is what a tooltip
// on a control is for. Same split for the game-owned bind rows, whose right-click RESETS to the
// factory key rather than clearing (and now says so), and for the wheel slider, whose explanation
// joined its description while its LIVE notch readout moved INSIDE it, where the updater's
// textContent can no longer eat the text. The only hover description left in the three panes is the
// category header's, kept as data in MX_SECTION_DESC beside the colour map, so a new category
// cannot ship without one.
// Quick-chat's macro rows explain themselves the same way: their fixed Alt+digit bind and the
// reason it is fixed now sit behind their own arrow, and the keycap's tooltip is just the key.
// No feature row in any of the three panes answers a hover with prose any more.
// The arrow also had to be PLACED, not merely appended: in a flex row a description set to
// flex:1 1 100% wraps onto its own line and strands the arrow after it, and in a grid
// auto-placement only ever moves forward, so the third cell has to be NAMED. Both shapes are
// explicit now - order for the flex rows, grid-column for .mx-kb and its macro variant.
//
// (2) THE COUNT ON A CATEGORY HEADER IS THE REAL NUMBER OF ROWS. It was whatever the builder
// happened to know, except in Settings, where it was EVERY child of the section body, the
// explanation paragraphs included: "HUD & Windows" read 26 for 18 rows, "Camera & Zoom" 16 for 12,
// "Colors & Menu" 7 for 6, "Pattern" 8 for 3. One painter derives it from the DOM now - direct
// children that are feature rows, skipping the ones a filter hid - and it runs after every pane
// build and after every filter change, so while a search is active the number is the MATCH count: a
// category showing two rows says two. Hotkeys paints from the DOM as well, so all three panes
// answer the same way for the same reason.
//
// (3) "ENABLE CUSTOM THEME" IS BACK, AT THE TOP OF THEMES. v1.30.0 deleted it because a saved
// false silently made the whole pane do nothing; the answer is not to hide the switch again but to
// make OFF loud. It sits above the search bar, so it can never be filtered, folded or dragged
// away, and with it off the rows stay EDITABLE - a look can be set up before it is switched on -
// while the pane dims and the master row says in words that the rows below do nothing. A stored
// boolean is honoured either way and the migration no longer forces it back on at the next reload,
// which is the part that made the old switch untrustworthy.
//
// (4) THE ACTION BUTTONS WEAR A WHITE OUTLINE THAT COMPLETELY SURROUNDS THEM. A 1.5px ring just
// outside the button pulses (opacity and box-shadow, so there is motion on all four sides
// everywhere), and a white arc sweeps the whole perimeter through a registered custom property -
// which is what makes the GRADIENT itself animatable; rotating the pseudo-element instead would
// rotate its mask and take the ring's rounded corners with it. Reduce effects keeps the ring and
// stops the sweep.
//
// (5) THE PROFILE CARD'S PARTICLE FIELD IS FITTED, NOT ASSUMED. The backing store used to be read
// once at start (459x379 for a 457x377 card), so it ignored devicePixelRatio and never followed a
// window resize, a menu-scale change or a taller pane: soft on any retina display, stretched for
// the rest of the session. It is sized to the real box at the real ratio, re-fitted on a slow
// cadence from the field's own frame, and the live motes are rescaled with it so none is stranded
// off-canvas. It also grew up - dust plus soft glow ORBS drawn from one cached radial-gradient
// sprite and twinkling SPARKS, a parallax that pulls the field against the pointer by depth, and a
// click burst of short-lived motes where the pointer landed. And it watches its own cost: if the
// frames it is drawn in get slow it DEMOTES itself (fewer motes, smaller bursts) and recovers when
// they are fast again, because a decoration must never be the reason the menu stutters.
// __murther.particles() reports the fitted box, the ratio it drew at, the per-kind budget and
// whether it demoted - so "is it blurry" and "is it heavy" are measurements, not opinions.
// Themes rows also moved into their section BODY: those categories finally FOLD (they hid an empty
// box before) and every row gets the card look v1.52.0 wrote for them, which until now matched no
// element in that pane at all.
//
// v1.54.1: Auto Reverse's four dead paths are live, and each fix is the honest one. The fanout no
// longer dispatches synthetic key events the engine is known to ignore - it sends the real
// 1-byte split frames (op 0x11) through the tracked game socket, the boundary the reverse
// rewrite already owns, with splitDelayMs between presses and anti-freeze closing the window
// and releasing the lock if a send throws. The mode -> split mapping is written ONCE
// (1/2/3/4/6 presses reach 2/4/8/16/64 cells; the player's own trusted press is the first,
// so 64x sends 5 extra frames where the old double map sent 4). Bind presses call
// fireTrigger directly - a bind is an explicit user action, so the trigger mode governs the
// AUTOMATIC detector only, and a bind can never be dead in confirmed mode again; the press
// asks for a fresh aim lock through the call, never through parked state. The automatic
// detector finally SEES the frames it classifies: the send wrapper inspects the 1-byte op
// 0x11 shape beside the 9-byte tick, and isSplitTick tests the split frame's own shape
// instead of the tick's 9 bytes, which nothing split-shaped can satisfy. 'Confirmed' now
// means confirmed: a split is only counted ignored when the scene read shows the 16-cell cap
// already reached (prevCellCount was never fed before), so plain second presses stay
// 'predictive'. aimTick only reads a vector out of 9-byte ticks. Solo trick holds its lock
// through the rollout and releases it when the window closes. The Auto Reverse tab's mode row and
// diagnostics name the new mechanism, and fanout frames sent joins the counters.
//
// v1.55.2: the shipped backup is the factory setup now. DEFAULTS carries the
// reference backup's values, so a fresh install (or a Factory reset) opens with:
// mono-purple accents (#a855f7/#a855f7), full panel opacity, menu scale 0.93,
// Auto Respawn on, arena border off, Zoom-speed boost already pushed, Retina
// quality with antialias off, animation delay 35, chat IDs + colors on, linesplit
// in toggle mode, Quick chat starting expanded, and bound keys (K/N/Semicolon/G/U
// for the game display actions, Digit1-3 for Auto Reverse 1x/4x/8x - conflicts, if any,
// surface per row in Hotkeys). game.zoomSpeed's stored default is the backup's
// boolean until the native sync resolves the live percent within a tick. Saved
// installs are untouched: stored values always win over DEFAULTS.
//
// v1.55.1: opening the client shows the menu ALONE - while the lobby menu is open
// (a dedicated body.mx-lobby-menu class, toggled by applyLobbyHud/setMenu and NOT
// gated on the lobby toggles) the arena canvas is visibility-hidden (never
// display:none, so the engine's layout box survives), Murther's fx overlay, every
// HUD wrapper (chat included) and ads/top banners are suppressed, and the page is
// painted opaque. Opening the menu mid-match still keeps the blurred arena behind
// it - this is lobby-only.
//
// v1.56.0: the leaderboard is redesigned for readability in the client's own
// style (the Ryuten grey slabs, free-flow tokens and filler rule read as one flat
// list - rank, name and score drifted per row and the dim rule looked like a stray
// divider). Rows are now real RANK / NAME / SCORE columns: a fixed badge column
// (gold / silver / bronze pills for the podium, muted ink otherwise), a fixed
// right-aligned tabular score column, and the name ellipsised into what is left
// (the full top-10 rides the board's hover tooltip, so nothing is lost). Slabs are
// the client's purple-tinted glass with a zebra step; your row keeps the Themes
// "Own row highlight" colour as tint, bar, frame and name plus a YOU pill, party
// rows keep the party colour, and the filler rule is gone. A slim LEADERBOARD
// header (live count) sits above the board; the Reset / Time / Spec footer below
// it is untouched, as are the place-change ghosts, washes and delta chips.
//
// v1.56.1: Esc no longer desyncs the client from the game. The key was only
// preventDefaulted, so the game's own Escape listener still fired underneath and
// showed the native #main: the client then read "lobby" (body.mx-lobby hid every
// panel but the chat and the M chip) while the game sat in its menu with the
// camera frozen and no Murther menu to press Play in. Esc is now swallowed in
// capture before the game sees it (keydown plus the keyup of the same press),
// including while typing (blurs instead of leaving) and on repeat; Esc with the
// menu open always closes it even when the Menu hotkey was rebound; and a
// poll-based heal reopens the menu if the game ever shows #main while Murther's
// menu is closed after a live match, with the death/join watchers re-armed when
// the game replaces #main from under them.
//
// v1.56.2: the leaderboard goes simple - the v1.56.0 podium treatment (medal
// pills, YOU tag, count header) was too busy, so the header bar is gone, the
// rank column is gone and each row is just NAME left and the game's own
// per-player MASS right ("19.5k" style, the mass token the game itself draws on
// that row, so every player's mass is tracked, never invented) on quiet
// alternating bands, with long names ellipsised and the full standings kept on
// the board's hover tooltip. Your row and party rows keep only their tint +
// edge bar, and the place-change ghosts, washes, delta chips and the Reset /
// Time / Spec footer are untouched. The card itself wears the client's accent
// as a travelling border beam - the Play/Spectate ring mechanism (conic
// gradient masked to a 1.5px ring, driven by the registered --mx-ring angle),
// frozen under Reduce effects / reduced-motion.
//
// v1.57.0: the console finally answers "is Pixi even here" - and the answer is that a
// SILENT console was never evidence of anything. Game Files/BwavBt3c.js IS PixiJS 8.19.0
// and it calls both dev hooks this client installs (__PIXI_APP_INIT__ and
// __PIXI_RENDERER_INIT__, each with the version string "8.19.0" as its second argument),
// but v8 registers its hello system with defaultOptions { hello: false }, so the greeting
// only prints when the renderer was created with `hello: true` - which this game never asks
// for. The "Pixi.js 4.2.2 - WebGL" banner everyone remembers came from a build that asked
// for it BY DEFAULT (SenpaiAgarOnline Files/pixi_v4.2.2.min.js). Nothing is installed or
// bundled here: Pixi belongs to the page, and a second copy would own no canvas.
// What changes instead is that the client reports its own engine state:
//  - one boot line per load (client version + host), then the engine line the moment a hook
//    fires - the version Pixi hands the hook (the old wire() DROPPED that second argument,
//    which is why nothing could ever say 8.19.0), the renderer's name / type /
//    context.webGLVersion and the canvas it draws into, i.e. the fields Pixi's own greeting
//    uses, read from the captured object;
//  - and, when no hook ever fires, the REASON rather than a shrug: a 2D canvas engine
//    (Pixi's CanvasRenderer registers no Application-system and no WebGL/WebGPU hook at
//    all) is named as such, from the fillText capture's own per-canvas counters - never by
//    probing getContext, which would CREATE a 2D context on the arena canvas and break the
//    engine's WebGL - and a build whose renderer has simply not been created yet says so
//    instead of implying a broken install;
//  - the hooks stop CLAIMING the global: the previous owner is chained (the official Pixi
//    DevTools extension installs that very name, so both tools can now see the app) and a
//    global another script took is re-claimed on the 1s pass;
//  - the engine is named where it matters: the Performance note, its capture-hook and
//    page-build rows, a Copy build stamp button, and __murther.pixi().
// Two ports of the diagnostics a companion client prints ride along, both behind ONE new
// switch - Settings > Performance > "Diagnostics logging", off by default, which touches
// nothing but console lines (it lives there because v1.58.0 removed the Developer console
// category that would otherwise have owned it): a build stamp (client version, engine, and the
// bundles this page really loads - measured, never typed by hand) and the game socket's
// connection lifecycle - connecting / socket opened with the handshake time / socket closed
// with the SERVER's own reason verbatim ("Keepalive timeout" is one this game really sends)
// / socket error. Third-party sockets (Firebase and friends) stay silent, because the same
// classification that owns the loss meter owns the log.
//
 // v1.58.0: the Developer console category is gone, completely and permanently - menu
// section, anti-debug engine (cleaner, scanner, overrides installer, freeze guard,
// notices, fix card), console API (devtools/devtoolsScan/antidebug) and every test and
// tool around them. The settings keys (devguard/dtcDone/dtAutoArm) are dropped, the
// hook's stall guard treats every long stall as genuine again, and the harness asserts
// the trap fields stay ABSENT so the feature cannot come back unnoticed. Generic
// Diagnostics logging (console only) is untouched.
//
// v1.59.0: Mass/Score sync is forced back. The id read (#pmass/#pscore/#playerScore,
// #pcells/#pid) matched a markup this build does not ship - Game Files/BcFPFY2o.js
// k074/k080/k081/k082 and Ch0q9gw8.js carry NO ids, just "ID:<span>0", "Mass: 0",
// "Score: 0", "Cells: 0/16" - so native-by-id had 0 hits, moved stayed false, the
// frozen-template gate refused the panel text, and the strip sat at "—" while the
// game ran. The resolver is now three-deep (legacy id, then the panel row whose
// LABEL names the key, then the canonical ID/FPS/Ping/Mass/Score/Cells child order),
// a value that is already non-zero on first sight counts as moved, the frozen check
// compares against the KNOWN zero template instead of whatever was seen first, the
// DOM hook also catches CharacterData.data/innerText writes, and an opt-out
// inbound WebSocket census trains on trusted DOM mass and then publishes net
// mass/score when the DOM goes quiet (3s expiry, never invented, __murther.netStats()).
//
// v1.60.0: the chat is rebuilt for reading, not repainted. Same DOM, same selectors,
// same game text - only type and rhythm: Inter stays the default face (System and Mono
// join it as choices), messages move to #e4e4ee at 1.6 line height with real row
// padding, hairline separators and hover on both paths (adopted native table and
// Murther's own fallback), the (id) token Murther wraps goes mono at 92% so IDs scan
// as data while nicks stay exactly as the game rendered them, tabs keep their
// typography and only inherit the font, and keyboard focus gets a visible ring.
// Three Settings rows own it live (Chat font / Chat message size 13-16px / Chat
// compact density), riding the Settings backup group with Inter / 14px / comfortable
// defaults so an untouched install looks as before; the input keeps its 15px size
// and only follows the font choice. mxChatTick, the in-place ID decorate, the unread
// badge, resize grips, opacity/blur theming and the animations switch are untouched.
//
// v1.60.1: the player bubble goes everywhere and gets compact. The chat
// right-click bubble is now a shared component: the leaderboard answers the
// same gesture with the same bubble, reading "Player · leaderboard" and
// showing Place + Mass + Server (the board knows no ID, so none is invented).
// The hit-test maps the pointer into the leaderboard canvas and takes the
// nearest captured game row; a miss just suppresses the menu. The bubble
// itself shrinks globally (168-228px, 28px avatar, tighter rows/buttons).
// Mention inserts @nick (never @@) and chat highlights mentions in place:
// any @token gets a subtle pill, your own nick the strong one, via the same
// safe text-node split as the ID decorator with its own data-mx-ment marker
// so old rows highlight without ever recounting as new.
//

// v1.60.2: the top-centre stats strip (ID / Mass / Score / Cells) opens BIGGER by
// default, because the first thing a new player reads should be readable at a
// glance. Both faces of the strip move TOGETHER so it can never render mixed
// sizes: Murther's own chips (.mx-stats>div) go from 12px type with 6x14 padding
// to 14px with 8x16, and the reskinned native #score-panel (the adopted face,
// pinned by !important rules) takes the same 14px / 8x16. The reserved value
// columns grow with the type - the panel's 56 -> 66px, the chips' inline 64 ->
// 74px - so live Mass/Score updates still expand into reserved space instead of
// re-flowing the strip (the v1.24.0 guarantee, kept at the larger size). About
// 25% more presence and nothing else moves: the strip is still centred with
// translateX(-50%), the game's own UI Scale option still stacks on top of the
// new base, the lobby / lobby-menu hiding and the self-heal are untouched, and
// every rule touched stays scoped to #murther-root or the strip's own classes -
// no page-wide style and no shared-state change, so other tabs and the
